## 8. Proposal-First Automation Architecture

**PLANNED FOR STEP 3 — NOT ACTIVE IN STEP 1 OR STEP 2**

All automation in iStaffing must follow the proposal-first pattern. Models MUST NOT directly write production schedule records.

### Proposal Lifecycle States

Every staffing proposal that passes through the automation layer must carry one of the following lifecycle states:

| State | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| draft | Proposal has been generated by iStaffing Scheduler; not yet validated |
| validation_failed | iStaffing Validator rejected the proposal; blocked from execution |
| validation_passed | iStaffing Validator approved the proposal; ready for approval gate |
| approval_required | Human approval is required before the proposal may be executed |
| approved | Qualified human approver has reviewed and approved the proposal |
| executed | Proposal has been executed and a schedule record has been written |
| execution_failed | Execution attempt failed; proposal is in error state |
| notified | Relevant parties have been notified of the executed assignment |
| rolled_back | A previously executed proposal has been reversed; rollback is logged |

### Staffing Pipeline (Step 3 Design — NOT Active)

```
[Shift Need Identified]
        ↓
[iStaffing Scheduler: draft proposal]
        ↓
[iStaffing Validator: validate proposal]
        ↓  (if validation_failed → block, surface reason to human)
[validation_passed]
        ↓
[iStaffing Prime: classify risk, prepare summary]
        ↓
[approval_required → Human approval gate]
        ↓  (qualified human approver reviews and approves)
[approved]
        ↓
[Deterministic execution layer writes schedule record]
        ↓
[executed → notified → audit log entry]
        ↓  (if execution_failed or rollback needed → rolled_back → logged)
```

No step in this pipeline may be bypassed. No model role may approve its own proposal. Human approval is required before any production schedule record is written.

